Carry the OpenBao storage index from a write to its reads

Barbican's vault plugin (barbican/plugin/vault_secret_store.py) stores
and reads secret payloads through castellan's VaultKeyManager, which
sends every request with the token header only. OpenBao answers reads
on standby nodes (`disable_standby_reads` is off by default), and a
standby that has not applied the latest Raft entries answers the read
of a secret written milliseconds earlier with 404. castellan raises
`ManagedObjectNotFoundError`, `CastellanSecretStore.get_secret` turns
it into `SecretGeneralException`, and the API answers HTTP 500 ("Secret
payload retrieval failure seen"). On a ControlPlane of release 2026.1
with three Barbican API replicas and a three-replica OpenBao 2.6.3,
1,464 of 44,913 payload downloads in 24 hours failed (3.3 %). For one
secret `PUT /metadata` returned 201, `GET /payload` returned 500 25 ms
later, and the same GET returned 200 278 ms after that (issue
C5C3/cobaltcore#1703).

OpenBao 2.7 answers a request that changed storage with an
`X-Vault-Index` header that encodes the cluster ID and the storage
index of the change. A standby that receives the index together with
`X-Vault-Inconsistent: forward-active-node` handles the request itself
once it has applied the index, and forwards it to the active node
before. A request carries one index at most; a request without one is
handled by whichever node receives it, and an index of another cluster
is ignored. The write and the read of a secret run in different API
processes, so the index travels in the database: the patch records it
in the `vault_index` row of `secret_store_metadata`, next to the
`key_id` the plugin already stores there.

- `_StorageIndexAdapter`, a requests `HTTPAdapter` that
  `VaultSecretStore.__init__` mounts for `https://` and `http://` on
  the key manager's session, records the `X-Vault-Index` of the
  response to every `POST` in a `threading.local` state, and sends an
  index with `X-Vault-Inconsistent: forward-active-node` when the state
  holds one.
- `VaultSecretStore._meta_dict` adds the index of the last write to
  the metadata that `store_secret`, `generate_symmetric_key` and
  `generate_asymmetric_key` build after their last write; both dicts of
  a key pair carry the index of the second write, which covers the
  first. An index longer than the 255 characters of the `value` column,
  or one that is not printable ASCII without whitespace, is not
  recorded, and one WARNING names the key ID and the reason: the first
  would fail the database write, the second every later read, because
  requests refuses to send such a header value.
- `VaultSecretStore.get_secret` sets the secret's index for the
  requests of the read and clears it in a `finally` block, so a failed
  read leaves no index for the next request on the thread.
- castellan runs an AppRole login on the same session right before a
  request whenever its cached token is missing or expired. A successful
  login (`POST`, status 200, a path under `/v1/auth/` that ends in
  `/login`) answers with an index too, and the request right after the
  login carries that index in place of the secret's: OpenBao takes a
  single index, and the login's is the later one, because every index a
  caller holds was issued before the login ran.
- A key manager without a requests session leaves the tracking off,
  with one WARNING ("Vault storage index tracking is disabled").

Against a server that returns no index (OpenBao before 2.7, HashiCorp
Vault Community) no `vault_index` row is written and requests go out as
before. Secrets stored before the patch have no row and are read as
before. An image without the patch ignores the row, so a rollback of
the image needs no data change.

The patch imports requests without a requirements.txt entry: castellan
requires it, and hack/nix-lock.sh refuses a patch that edits
requirements.txt.

Tests: no upstream test moves. `WhenTestingVaultSecretStore` in
barbican/tests/plugin/test_castellan_secret_store.py builds a real
`VaultSecretStore` and swaps its key manager for a mock; a fresh store
holds no index, so the metadata those tests compare stays the same. The
patch adds barbican/tests/plugin/test_vault_secret_store.py, twenty-one
tests that replace requests' `HTTPAdapter.send` with a mock and need no
server: the index each write path records, no index for a missing or
empty header or for a write after one that returned an index, the
length and character checks with their WARNING, the headers of a read
with and without an index, the login index on the first request after a
login and the secret's on the next, a write right after a login that
carries the login's index and records its own, no login index after a
refused login, no index left behind after a read that failed with a 404
or a connection error, no index that a read on one thread lends to a
write on another, and a key manager without a session. All twenty-one
fail without the patch.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_barbican.sh (Test 10) drives
`_StorageIndexAdapter.send` in the built image through a write and a
read, and fails on the import without this patch.

The 2026.2 twin carries the same code and tests; the context of the
vault_secret_store.py hunks differs, because 23.0.0 adds the `kv_path`
and `kv_version` options.

Applies to barbican 22.0.0 (2026.1), on top of 0001 and 0002.

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

